<mjml>
  <mj-head>
    <mj-title>Anthias Newsletter</mj-title>

    <!-- Preview text shown next to the subject line in the inbox.
         Keep it under ~90 characters or clients truncate it. -->
    <mj-preview>Your player now warns you when the hardware is in trouble.</mj-preview>

    <!--
      Plus Jakarta Sans is vendored locally for the web app, but email
      cannot load /static/ fonts, so it is pulled from Google Fonts
      here. Only Apple Mail and a few others honour it. Everything else
      falls back to Helvetica/Arial, which is why the stack below is
      always spelled out in full rather than relying on the @font-face.
    -->
    <mj-font
      name="Plus Jakarta Sans"
      href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Plus+Jakarta+Sans:wght@400;700&amp;display=swap" />

    <!--
      RESOLVED TOKENS
      ===============
      Email clients strip CSS custom properties, and Outlook drops
      rgba(), so no token from the design system can be used here by
      name: every one has to arrive as a literal. That makes this file
      a second copy of the token layer, and a second copy goes stale
      silently. It already did once, when the token authority moved out
      of src/anthias_server/app/static/sass/_variables.scss
      and into the @theme block in
      src/anthias_server/app/static/src/tailwind.css, and every
      value below kept rendering the colours of a file that no longer
      existed.

      So the table is the only place a literal may be written, and
      tests/test_email_tokens.py re-derives every row from the real
      token layer and fails if a row drifts. It also fails on any
      literal used further down that is not a value in this table, so
      the table cannot be routed around.

      Rows read: TOKEN [on BACKDROP] = literal.
      A backdrop is named when the token is declared with alpha; the
      literal is that colour composited over it, since a translucent
      value has no meaning without one. Lengths are the rem token
      multiplied by the 16px root.

      Line heights are not listed. Every one of them belongs to a step
      of the type ramp, so instead of a row per value the test pairs
      each font-size in this file with its line-height and checks that
      the two are the same step: --text-lg with --text-lg--line-height,
      never a size from one step beside a leading from another.

      Light theme only. The web app themes off a data-theme attribute
      written before first paint, which email has no equivalent of, so
      the newsletter renders the theme the app ships by default. Canvas
      and chrome are brand furniture and do not invert anyway, so the
      frame reads the same either way.

        --color-canvas                              = #270035
        --color-on-canvas                           = #ffffff
        --color-on-canvas-muted  on --color-canvas  = #b3a6b8
        --color-on-canvas-faint  on --color-canvas  = #9e8ca4

        --color-surface                             = #ffffff
        --color-surface-tint                        = #f6f3f8
        --color-fg                                  = #1f002a
        --color-fg-muted                            = #5e5868
        --color-divider          on --color-surface = #ebebeb

        --color-feature-to                          = #492955
        --color-on-feature                          = #ffffff

      The footer hairline, and the one row that names a primitive
      rather than a role. There is no divider role for the canvas
      plane because the app has no hairline sitting directly on the
      canvas; sass/_styles.scss draws its footer rule on the chrome
      veil and reaches for a --color-paper-* rung there too.

        --color-paper-12         on --color-canvas  = #411f4d

        --color-accent                              = #ffe11a
        --color-accent-text                         = #1f002a

        --text-2xs                                  = 11px
        --text-xs                                   = 12px
        --text-sm                                   = 14px
        --text-base                                 = 16px
        --text-lg                                   = 18px
        --text-xl                                   = 20px
        --text-3xl                                  = 30px
        --tracking-wider                            = 0.1em
        --radius-sm                                 = 4px
        --radius-md                                 = 8px
        --radius-lg                                 = 12px

      Three deliberate departures from the app, none of which a token
      can express in email:

      1. The primary button is --gradient-accent in the app. Gradients
         do not render in Outlook or in most webmail, so it is filled
         with --color-accent, the ramp's mid stop.
      2. The feature card is a gradient from --color-feature-from to
         --color-feature-to. Same reason; it is filled with the -to
         stop, the darker of the two, so --color-on-feature keeps the
         contrast it was measured at.
      3. Body copy on the canvas uses --color-on-canvas-muted rather
         than --color-fg-muted. --color-fg* is text on a SURFACE and
         flips with the theme; text sitting directly on the plum canvas
         is always light. Reaching for the wrong one of that pair is
         the most likely mistake in this file.
    -->
    <mj-attributes>
      <mj-all font-family="'Plus Jakarta Sans', Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif" />

      <mj-section padding="0" />
      <mj-column padding="0" />

      <mj-text
        color="#1f002a"
        font-size="16px"
        line-height="1.5"
        padding="0" />

      <mj-button
        background-color="#ffe11a"
        color="#1f002a"
        font-size="16px"
        font-weight="700"
        border-radius="8px"
        inner-padding="14px 32px"
        padding="0" />

      <!-- Text roles. Applied with mj-class on the element. -->
      <mj-class name="eyebrow"
        color="#ffe11a" font-size="12px" font-weight="700"
        letter-spacing="0.1em" text-transform="uppercase" />
      <mj-class name="h1"
        color="#ffffff" font-size="30px" font-weight="700"
        line-height="1.2" />
      <mj-class name="h2"
        color="#1f002a" font-size="20px" font-weight="700"
        line-height="1.35" />
      <mj-class name="h3"
        color="#1f002a" font-size="18px" font-weight="700"
        line-height="1.4" />
      <mj-class name="lead"
        color="#b3a6b8" font-size="18px" line-height="1.4" />
      <mj-class name="body-copy"
        color="#1f002a" font-size="16px" line-height="1.5" />
      <mj-class name="muted"
        color="#5e5868" font-size="14px" line-height="1.45" />
      <mj-class name="on-feature"
        color="#ffffff" font-size="14px" line-height="1.45" />
      <mj-class name="footer-text"
        color="#b3a6b8" font-size="12px" line-height="1.4" />
      <mj-class name="footer-faint"
        color="#9e8ca4" font-size="11px" line-height="1.45" />

      <!-- Planes -->
      <mj-class name="canvas" background-color="#270035" />
      <mj-class name="surface" background-color="#ffffff" />
      <mj-class name="feature" background-color="#492955" />

      <mj-class name="rule-light" border-color="#ebebeb" border-width="1px" />
      <mj-class name="rule-dark" border-color="#411f4d" border-width="1px" />
    </mj-attributes>

    <!-- The width at which the two-up block stops being two-up. This is
         MJML's default, stated so the media query below and the stacking
         it compensates for cannot drift apart. -->
    <mj-breakpoint width="480px" />

    <mj-style>
      /*
        The gutter between the two cards belongs to the LEFT column's
        right edge, not split across both. Padding on the right column
        survives stacking, where it stops being a gutter and becomes an
        indent under the card above it.

        What stacking does lose is the vertical gap, so the second
        column takes one back below the breakpoint only.
      */
      @media only screen and (max-width: 480px) {
        .stacked-gap { padding-top: 24px !important; }
      }

      /*
        Anchors inside mj-text, which mj-class cannot reach.

        Both of these are on a dark plane, where the brand yellow is a
        usable link colour. There is deliberately no rule for a link on
        a white surface: the current copy has none, and the app answers
        that case with --color-link rather than the yellow, which has
        far too little contrast against white. Adding one means adding
        that token to the table above first.
      */
      a.link-on-dark { color: #ffe11a; text-decoration: underline; }
      a.link-footer { color: #b3a6b8; text-decoration: underline; }

      code {
        background-color: #f6f3f8;
        border-radius: 4px;
        padding: 2px 6px;
        font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, Consolas, monospace;
        font-size: 14px;
      }
    </mj-style>
  </mj-head>

  <mj-body background-color="#270035" width="640px">

    <!--
      Masthead. Gmail, Outlook and Yahoo all refuse to render SVG, so
      website/assets/images/logo-full.svg cannot be used directly and
      website/static/img/anthias-logo-email.png is shipped for this
      alone. It is drawn at 2x and displayed at half that, so it stays
      sharp on a retina screen.

      It is also flattened onto --color-canvas rather than left
      transparent, because a transparent PNG picks up a black matte in
      older Outlook builds. That bakes the canvas colour into an image
      file, so test_email_tokens.py checks the PNG's own background
      against the token too.
    -->
    <mj-section mj-class="canvas" padding="32px 32px 24px">
      <mj-column>
        <mj-image
          src="https://anthias.screenly.io/img/anthias-logo-email.png"
          alt="Anthias"
          width="179px"
          align="left"
          padding="0" />
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>

    <!-- Hero -->
    <mj-section mj-class="canvas" padding="8px 32px 40px">
      <mj-column>
        <mj-text mj-class="eyebrow" padding="0 0 12px">
          Release {{var:release_version:"2026.08.2"}}
        </mj-text>
        <mj-text mj-class="h1" padding="0 0 16px">
          Your player tells you when the hardware is in trouble
        </mj-text>
        <mj-text mj-class="lead">
          Hello {{var:first_name:"there"}}, here is what changed in
          Anthias this month, and what it means for the screens you
          already have running.
        </mj-text>
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>

    <!-- Main content card -->
    <mj-section mj-class="surface" border-radius="12px" padding="40px 32px">
      <mj-column>
        <mj-text mj-class="h2" padding="0 0 16px">
          Two new warnings, and both say what to do next
        </mj-text>
        <mj-text mj-class="body-copy" padding="0 0 24px">
          The web interface now tells you when the power supply cannot
          hold its voltage, and when the storage card starts developing
          bad blocks. Until now the only sign of either was the screen
          itself misbehaving, which is a hard thing to trace back to a
          cable or a card. Each notice says what is wrong and what to do
          about it rather than reporting a fault, and both are readable
          on every screen and in dark mode.
        </mj-text>

        <mj-button align="left"
                   href="https://github.com/Screenly/Anthias/releases/tag/v2026.08.2">
          Read the release notes
        </mj-button>

        <mj-divider mj-class="rule-light" padding="32px 0" />

        <mj-text mj-class="h3" padding="0 0 8px">
          One design system behind the interface
        </mj-text>
        <mj-text mj-class="muted" padding="0 0 24px">
          Color, spacing and type now come from a single set of design
          tokens rather than from whichever page you happen to be on.
          Nothing moves, but everything lines up, and it holds in both
          light and dark mode.
        </mj-text>

        <mj-text mj-class="h3" padding="0 0 8px">
          Smaller fixes you will notice
        </mj-text>
        <mj-text mj-class="muted" padding="0 0 24px">
          Field labels in the Edit asset dialog no longer sit on top of
          their example text. Turning off certificate verification now
          shows a "Not secure" badge, so it is obvious at a glance. The
          disk and memory charts use the same color for the same thing.
        </mj-text>

        <mj-text mj-class="h3" padding="0 0 8px">
          How to get it
        </mj-text>
        <mj-text mj-class="muted">
          Run <code>./bin/upgrade_containers.sh</code> on the device.
          Balena fleets pick the release up on their own.
        </mj-text>
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>

    <!-- The cards are full-bleed, so nothing separates them but this.
         An mj-spacer rather than padding on either card: padding inside
         a section with a background is filled by that background, which
         would close the gap it was added to open. -->
    <mj-section mj-class="canvas">
      <mj-column>
        <mj-spacer height="16px" />
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>

    <!-- Two-up secondary block -->
    <mj-section mj-class="feature" border-radius="12px" padding="32px">
      <mj-column width="50%" padding="0 24px 0 0">
        <mj-text mj-class="eyebrow" padding="0 0 8px">From the docs</mj-text>
        <mj-text mj-class="on-feature">
          <a class="link-on-dark" href="https://anthias.screenly.io/docs/balena-fleet-deployment/">
            Deploying a Balena fleet
          </a><br />
          Updating every screen you run without touching them one by one.
        </mj-text>
      </mj-column>
      <mj-column width="50%" css-class="stacked-gap">
        <mj-text mj-class="eyebrow" padding="0 0 8px">Community</mj-text>
        <mj-text mj-class="on-feature">
          <a class="link-on-dark" href="https://forums.screenly.io/c/anthias/">
            The Anthias forum
          </a><br />
          Questions, hardware reports, and what people are building.
        </mj-text>
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>

    <!-- Footer -->
    <mj-section mj-class="canvas" padding="40px 32px 16px">
      <mj-column>
        <mj-divider mj-class="rule-dark" padding="0 0 24px" />
        <mj-text mj-class="footer-text" padding="0 0 16px">
          <a class="link-footer" href="https://anthias.screenly.io/">Website</a>
          &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp;
          <a class="link-footer" href="https://anthias.screenly.io/docs/">Documentation</a>
          &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp;
          <a class="link-footer" href="https://github.com/Screenly/Anthias">GitHub</a>
        </mj-text>
        <mj-text mj-class="footer-faint" padding="0 0 12px">
          Anthias is free and open source software, maintained by
          Screenly and its contributors.
        </mj-text>
        <mj-text mj-class="footer-faint" padding="0 0 12px">
          Screenly Inc, 16192 Coastal Hwy., Lewes, DE 19958, United States
        </mj-text>
        <mj-text mj-class="footer-faint">
          You are receiving this because you signed up for Anthias
          release notes.
          <a class="link-footer" href="[[UNSUB_LINK_EN]]">Unsubscribe</a>
        </mj-text>
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>

  </mj-body>
</mjml>
